{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ThreeRowSignCrossing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ThreeRowSignCrossing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Instances.Real.Lemmas","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Order.IntermediateValue","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.two_block_neg_iff_ratio_gt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.two_block_neg_iff_ratio_gt_one","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ThreeRowSignCrossing.lean#L112-L122","doc":"**Sign of a two-block sum is the sign of `1 − r`.**\n\nThe companion readout used by the probe: with `T₁ > 0`, the sum `T₁ + T₂` is\nnegative precisely when the overtake ratio `r = (-T₂)/T₁` exceeds `1`.  This is\nwhat makes \"the `(3,2,1)` block has overtaken `(4,1,1)`\" and \"the total has gone\nnegative\" the *same* statement rather than two correlated observations. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.crossing_unique_of_strictMonoOn_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.crossing_unique_of_strictMonoOn_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ThreeRowSignCrossing.lean#L85-L110","doc":"**The crossing is unique when the block ratio is strictly monotone.**\n\nIn the two-block regime (`F = T₁ + T₂` with `T₁ > 0` and `T₂ < 0` throughout),\nwrite the *overtake ratio* `r = (-T₂)/T₁`.  Then `F q = 0` exactly when\n`r q = 1`, so if `r` is strictly monotone on `[a, b]` the crossing is unique.\n\nThis is the formal content of the word \"overtake\": the second block does not\nmerely become large, it grows *relative to* the first, and it does so\nmonotonically, so it crosses the first exactly once. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sign_crossing_of_block_dominance_exchange","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sign_crossing_of_block_dominance_exchange","kind":"theorem","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ThreeRowSignCrossing.lean#L46-L83","doc":"**Block dominance exchange forces a zero.**\n\nLet `F = T₁ + T₂ + R` be a continuous function split into two distinguished\nblocks and a remainder.  Suppose that\n\n* at the left endpoint `a` the first block is positive and **dominates**\n  everything else: `|T₂ a + R a| < T₁ a`;\n* at the right endpoint `b` the second block is negative and **dominates**\n  everything else: `|T₁ b + R b| < -T₂ b`.\n\nThen `F` vanishes somewhere strictly between `a` and `b`.\n\nThis is the exact shape of the measured deuteron data: `T₁` is the `(4,1,1)`\nterm, `T₂` the `(3,2,1)` term, and `R` the remaining ≥3-row blocks.  The\nhypotheses are falsifiable statements about those numbers — dropping either\ndominance hypothesis makes the conclusion fail (a positive `F` with a merely\n*larger* negative block elsewhere need not vanish). ","signatureChunk":0}]}